WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.835 --> 00:00:04.515
CAM software turns the 3D model into a toolpath.

2
00:00:05.236 --> 00:00:08.348
That is the route of the tool, with feeds and speeds.

3
00:00:09.367 --> 00:00:12.246
The programmer chooses the path feature by feature.

4
00:00:12.996 --> 00:00:15.492
A pocket gets an adaptive roughing path.

5
00:00:16.206 --> 00:00:17.959
A hole gets a drilling cycle.

6
00:00:18.671 --> 00:00:20.668
A face gets a finishing pass.

7
00:00:21.705 --> 00:00:24.805
Then a post-processor translates that toolpath into the

8
00:00:24.863 --> 00:00:28.288
G-code dialect of one specific machine and control.

9
00:00:29.283 --> 00:00:31.059
Same part, two machines.

10
00:00:31.796 --> 00:00:34.350
That means two posts and two programs.

11
00:00:35.380 --> 00:00:39.606
A bad post is a hidden crash risk in every program it writes.

12
00:00:40.907 --> 00:00:45.296
A simulation runs the posted G-code on a digital copy of the machine,

13
00:00:45.493 --> 00:00:48.256
with the fixture, the holders and the raw part.

14
00:00:49.319 --> 00:00:53.592
You see collisions, cuts into clamps and material left behind.

15
00:00:54.372 --> 00:00:57.530
That happens before the real machine sees the program.

16
00:00:58.529 --> 00:01:00.851
It is only as good as its models.

17
00:01:01.548 --> 00:01:06.726
A fixture drawn 10 mm too low gives a clean simulation and a real crash.

18
00:01:07.738 --> 00:01:11.894
Ask whether the simulation runs the real G-code after the post,

19
00:01:12.033 --> 00:01:13.740
or only the CAM path.

20
00:01:15.030 --> 00:01:18.037
Prove-out is the first real run of a new program.

21
00:01:18.716 --> 00:01:22.907
The machine runs in single block, so it stops after every line.

22
00:01:23.952 --> 00:01:25.752
Rapid moves are slowed down.

23
00:01:26.453 --> 00:01:28.729
Sometimes the machine cuts air first.

24
00:01:29.837 --> 00:01:32.554
Then comes the release after the first-off check.

25
00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:37.485
The first part is fully measured, and the program is released as proven.

26
00:01:38.494 --> 00:01:41.838
Edits made at the machine must go back into CAM.

27
00:01:42.549 --> 00:01:45.858
Otherwise the next repeat order starts with the old error.

28
00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:51.501
Good shops track how many hours prove-out takes per new program, and why.

29
00:01:52.515 --> 00:01:56.265
That shows whether the bottleneck is the programming or the trust in it.

30
00:01:57.625 --> 00:01:58.902
What a manager weighs.

31
00:01:59.628 --> 00:02:03.831
Programming time grows with complexity and with the number of new parts.

32
00:02:04.817 --> 00:02:08.689
For small batches the programming time can exceed the machine time.

33
00:02:09.678 --> 00:02:10.688
There are levers.

34
00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:13.321
Templates for recurring features.

35
00:02:14.055 --> 00:02:16.180
And a validated post per machine.

36
00:02:17.137 --> 00:02:18.681
Full-machine simulation.

37
00:02:19.418 --> 00:02:23.412
And a tool library in CAM that matches the real tool rack.

38
00:02:24.449 --> 00:02:25.738
Credible questions.

39
00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:30.419
How many hours of prove-out does each new program take on the machine?

40
00:02:31.411 --> 00:02:34.999
How many programs are edited at the machine and never fed back?

41
00:02:36.033 --> 00:02:38.448
Which machine has a post nobody trusts?

42
00:02:39.764 --> 00:02:43.967
A job shop writes about 150 new programs a year.

43
00:02:44.983 --> 00:02:47.642
Today CAM takes 4 hours per program.

44
00:02:48.331 --> 00:02:51.141
The programmer costs 70 euros an hour.

45
00:02:52.152 --> 00:02:55.263
Prove-out takes 2.5 hours on the machine.

46
00:02:55.964 --> 00:02:59.853
Machine and setter together cost 110 euros an hour.

47
00:03:00.644 --> 00:03:04.093
These are the shop’s own figures, stated as assumptions.

48
00:03:05.115 --> 00:03:08.343
The proposal is full-machine simulation software.

49
00:03:09.062 --> 00:03:12.859
An assumed 20,000 euros a year, including support.

50
00:03:13.918 --> 00:03:16.855
The shop expects prove-out to drop to 1 hour.

51
00:03:17.543 --> 00:03:22.535
CAM time then rises by 0.25 hours to run the simulation.

52
00:03:23.528 --> 00:03:27.058
What does it save a year, net of the extra programming time?

53
00:03:27.756 --> 00:03:30.136
And is it worth the 20,000 euros?

54
00:03:31.445 --> 00:03:37.703
The machine saves 1.5 hours times 150 times 110 euros.

55
00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:41.911
That is 24,750 euros.

56
00:03:42.909 --> 00:03:49.503
The programmer costs 0.25 hours times 150 times 70 euros extra.

57
00:03:50.257 --> 00:03:53.659
That is 2,625 euros.

58
00:03:54.674 --> 00:04:01.327
Net that is 22,125 euros a year, against 20,000 euros.

59
00:04:02.029 --> 00:04:07.370
On hours alone that is only 2,125 euros ahead.

60
00:04:08.404 --> 00:04:10.877
So the case rests on two other things.

61
00:04:11.591 --> 00:04:13.147
The crashes it prevents.

62
00:04:13.868 --> 00:04:20.126
And the 225 machine hours it frees, 1.5 times 150.

63
00:04:21.110 --> 00:04:23.850
Those hours only count if there is work for them.

64
00:04:24.545 --> 00:04:27.436
Ask for last year’s crash cost before deciding.
